{
  /**
   * Type-checking, including the tests.
   *
   * ## Why this is not `tsconfig.json`
   *
   * That one is the **build's**: `vite build` reads it to decide what to compile and
   * what declarations to emit, so a test file added to its `include` ends up in
   * `dist`. Which is why every package's `include` was `src/**` only — and why the
   * tests of twenty-two packages were never once read by the compiler.
   *
   * ## Why that matters
   *
   * A test is the thing that says the code is right. A test the compiler never reads
   * is a claim nothing checked: a field written on an object that has no such field
   * passed twenty-five green tests here (`hit.needs` on an `AuditHit`), and Word has
   * nineteen more of exactly that in one spec file.
   *
   * `rootDir` is the repository, not the package: a sibling package is imported
   * through `paths` and resolves to its **source**, which is outside this directory —
   * without this, tsc reports every cross-package import as being outside the root and
   * says nothing about the code. Inert otherwise, since nothing is emitted.
   *
   * Held to by `pnpm type-check:tests` — see `scripts/typecheck-tests.mjs`, which
   * carries the count each package is still working off.
   */
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": true,
    "rootDir": "../..",
    /**
     * `describe`, `it`, `vi` — several packages set `globals: true` in their vitest
     * config and their tests use them without importing. Named here so the compiler
     * knows the same names the runner does; without it a test file that runs is a
     * file that will not compile, which is the wrong way round.
     */
    "types": ["vitest/globals", "node"]
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*", "test/**/*"]
}
